  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nMid-level warm air advection across southwest WI is lifting an \narcing band of showers toward central late this evening. However, \nwith such a dry surface layer in place, don't anticipate more than a \nsprinkle reaching the ground overnight. As a surface low lifts \ncloser to the region Saturday an draws addtioanl moisture into the \nregion expect some isolated to scattered showers to develop during \nthe afternoon and evening. Rainfall amounts will be fairly light \nwith most places seeing only a few hundreths. High pressure then \nbuilds back in on Sunday returning dry conditions to the region to \nfinish the weekend. \n\nA slow moving, moisture-laden system arrives Monday afternoon/\nevening and sticks around into at least Thursday. Rain is likely \nMonday night into Tuesday (80-95%) with the initial surge of \nmoisture and forcing. Then chance/likely PoPs will continue into \nWednesday, then taper off on Thursday as the system finally exits to \nour east. Chances for exceeding 0.50 inch rain Monday Night through \nTuesday are 70-90% with chances for exceeding 1.00 inch between 70-\n80% northwest of the Fox Valley, and 40-60% over east-central WI. \nHigher end projections take rainfall to 1.5 to 2.25 inches. \nCertainly looking like a soaking rain at times. Some elevated \ninstability and impressive dynamics with the initial WAA surge \nMonday night to include some thunder, especially across central and \nnorth-central WI. \n\nTemperatures will trend back above normal this weekend, with highs \non Halloween mainly in the 50s and in the low to mid 60s on Sunday \nthrough Wednesday. Temps cool off behind the large system at the end \nof the week.",
